# A Guided Laboratory for Pruned Component Spectra

Begin with the central idea: a spectrum records the sizes at which a structure exists, and eventual periodicity says that its distant tail repeats. Explore a repeating residue pattern and test candidate translations.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

The finite check below makes the same comparison numerically. It illustrates a certificate on a selected window; the accompanying theorem explains when a certificate applies to the infinite tail.

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the closure argument</summary>

If two spectra have eventual periods $q_1$ and $q_2$, translation by $q_1q_2$ preserves each spectrum beyond the larger threshold. It therefore preserves their intersection and union. Complementation negates both sides of the membership equivalence, and induction gives finite-union closure. See the [semilinear set overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semilinear_set) for broader context.

</details>

## Counting many components with finite memory

A logical observer often needs exact counts only while they are small. Move the threshold and add component counts interactively.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the additive congruence proof</summary>

Two counts are equivalent when equal or both at least $q$. If both input comparisons are exact, their sums agree. Otherwise one corresponding pair is already at least $q$, and adding nonnegative counts keeps both outputs at least $q$. Applying this argument at every component type proves profile congruence. Background on graph components is available in this [connected-component introduction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Component_(graph_theory)).

</details>

## The boundary: an unrestricted cutoff can write any pattern

The final station shows why the cutoff itself needs regularity. The displayed rule leaves residual $0$ at powers of two and residual $1$ elsewhere, so shifting the base spectrum $\{0\}$ reproduces the target exactly.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the nonperiodicity proof</summary>

Given any proposed threshold $N$ and positive period $q$, choose $2^m>\max(N,q)$. Periodicity would place $2^m+q$ among the powers of two, but $2^m<2^m+q<2^{m+1}$. No power of two lies strictly between consecutive powers. Thus every proposed eventual period fails. Compare the elementary growth pattern in this [exponential-function overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_function).

</details>

## What to carry forward

Finite Boolean operations and saturated addition preserve finite-state information. Arbitrary input-dependent shifts do not. A future positive pruning theorem should therefore constrain the cutoff—eventual affine behavior modulo the spectrum's period is a natural first candidate.

{{algorithm:0}}

